The patch fixes normal method-style hooks but introduces a failure for valid callable-object `__init_subclass__` hooks by unconditionally using descriptor binding.

Review comment:

- [P2] Handle non-descriptor __init_subclass__ hooks — /home/g/wt/paramsight/active/review-branch/src/paramsight/aliasclassmethod.py:93-93
  This assumes every owner-defined `__init_subclass__` implements `__get__`, but Python also accepts callable objects in the class dict, such as `__init_subclass__ = functools.partial(...)` or a decorator returning a callable instance. In those classes, subclassing a `TypeVarValue`/`takes_alias` owner now raises `AttributeError` from this line instead of invoking the hook like native class creation; bind with `__get__` only when present and otherwise call the callable directly.
